WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.992
A self join is not a separate JOIN keyword.

00:00:02.992 --> 00:00:08.612
It is a regular join where the same table appears twice using different aliases.

00:00:08.612 --> 00:00:13.387
The aliases allow the two copies of the table to play different roles.

00:00:13.387 --> 00:00:18.357
Here, medical staff is referenced as s1 and s2.

00:00:18.357 --> 00:00:22.037
We match staff members who belong to the same department.

00:00:22.037 --> 00:00:27.167
We also require the first staff ID to be less than the second staff ID.

00:00:27.167 --> 00:00:32.757
That second rule prevents matching a person to themselves and prevents reverse duplicates such as Alice-Bob

00:00:32.757 --> 00:00:35.657
and Bob-Alice.

00:00:35.657 --> 00:00:42.447
Self joins are very useful when rows in one table need to be compared with other rows from the same table.

00:00:42.447 --> 00:00:48.722
In an employee table, one row might represent an employee and another row the manager.

00:00:48.722 --> 00:00:53.332
In a sales table, you could compare purchases from the same customer.

00:00:53.332 --> 00:00:58.907
In a hospital, you can compare one admission with a later admission for the same patient.

00:00:58.907 --> 00:01:01.767
The important idea is role labeling.

00:01:01.767 --> 00:01:06.442
Before writing a self join, decide what each alias represents.

00:01:06.442 --> 00:01:08.632
Earlier event and later event.

00:01:08.632 --> 00:01:12.672
Employee and manager. First purchase and next purchase.

00:01:12.672 --> 00:01:16.477
Good alias roles make the logic much easier to understand.
